<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Senior Juz</h1><p class="st-meta">1718 CE – 1848 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern division of the Kazakh people in Zhetysu, which suffered Dzungar and Kokandi rule before passing to Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> had long been grouped into three juz, or hordes, and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a> around 1718 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate</a> lost its unity, with each juz under its own khans. The Senior Juz occupied the south-east, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> to the Ili valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its tribes, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulat-tribe">Dulat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uysyn">Uysun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalairs">Jalayir</a>, were nomadic herders closely tied to the oasis cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>. They were hit hardest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> invasions of the 1720s and partly ruled by the Dzungars until the 1750s. The judge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tole-bi">Tole Bi</a>, one of three famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> biys, was a leading figure.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the late 18th century much of the Senior Juz fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tashkent-hakimate">Tashkent Hakimate</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-kokand">Khanate of Kokand</a>. From the 1820s its northern clans accepted Russian protection, and by 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had abolished the khanship, incorporating it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/senior-juz.jpg" alt="Senior Juz" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Wassily · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%96%D1%83%D0%B7.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1718 CE – 1848 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zholbarys Khan, Tole Bi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kazakh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Horde (Kazakh), Great Juz, Uly Juz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1783&amp;focus=senior-juz" aria-label="Senior Juz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6262 479 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Senior Juz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6645 897h0M6951 879h0M6741 986h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 735h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6582 735h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Kazakhstan. <a href="/atlas?year=1783&amp;focus=senior-juz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
